{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1785003508,"nonce":"1UgVES4SWCm9QkW8DnJ7EfS5Ujo=","title":"**Found while fixing 98ea804** (PR #78), deliberately left out of that fix as a design call.","message":"On the k8s backend a **failed** attempt's artifacts are never harvested at all: `drive_workspace` gates the harvest on `exit == 0`. So the scheduler's `ExecState::Failed` artifact-harvest branch (`scheduler.rs`, commented \"often THE evidence\") is **dead code** on k8s, contradicting ADR-0056 and the of-record tests.\n\nA failing test's JUnit XML, a crashed build's log bundle, a screenshot from a failed browser run — exactly the artifacts a human most wants — are silently discarded.\n\n**Why it is not a one-line change:** simply harvesting on failure re-introduces the uploaded-but-unindexed bug that #78 closed, because the `Failed{class: Step}` verdict would be reported before the egress sidecar settles. The fix has to withhold that verdict until settle completes, the same way #78 now withholds Succeeded — which means deciding what happens when a failed step's harvest itself fails or times out.\n\n**Acceptance sketch**\n- [ ] A step that exits non-zero still has its `/scarab/artifacts` harvested and indexed.\n- [ ] The `Failed` verdict is withheld until settle, bounded by the step-timeout backstop (never uploaded-but-unindexed).\n- [ ] A harvest failure on an already-failing step does not mask the original failure class.\n- [ ] Acceptance test: failing step publishes an artifact, run reports failed, artifact is listed and downloadable.","files":null}]}